67
00:10:17.690 --> 00:10:34.470
Reza Rassool: Virtual Private Knowledge. That's what VPK stands for. I think the diagram pictures, pictures speak more loudly than just the words, but the purpose of the work group is to research virtual Private Knowledge, such that

68
00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:36.540
Reza Rassool: a custodian, Bob.

69
00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:42.500
Reza Rassool: can scramble data before transmitting it to an untrusted service provider, SAM.

70
00:10:42.650 --> 00:10:48.110
Reza Rassool: Such that the data remains encrypted at rest and searchable in its scrambled form.

71
00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:50.270
Reza Rassool: Okay, simple as that.

72
00:10:51.890 --> 00:11:01.970
Reza Rassool: For about 40 years, the industry's been obsessing about the communication between Alice and Bob, and that a character called Eve

73
00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:12.380
Reza Rassool: would come in the middle and eavesdrop on the conversation, either, wiretapping the line or conducting a man-in-the-middle attack to,

74
00:11:13.570 --> 00:11:21.919
Reza Rassool: To compromise and, and, gain information on this, secure, this conversation here.

75
00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:25.280
Reza Rassool: We say that's a largely solved problem.

76
00:11:25.450 --> 00:11:40.380
Reza Rassool: From Alice's point of view, who might be a customer or a client of Bob, or a patient, Bob is the custodian of her private data, and she's allowed to query it and get responses.

77
00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:55.280
Reza Rassool: She doesn't know that Bob has actually outsourced his infrastructure to Sam. So Sam is a new character we have to introduce in the cast of characters of cybersecurity. Sam stands for Service and Malice.

78
00:11:56.750 --> 00:12:11.540
Reza Rassool: So, who could Sam be? Well, it's no… it's no accident that he could be like a Sam Altman, but it could actually be any, third-party provider where you're transmitting… you're… currently, you're transmitting your data in the clear to them.

79
00:12:11.740 --> 00:12:20.759
Reza Rassool: And so can we transmit the data scrambled, such that it remains scrambled at rest, but still searchable?

80
00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:21.610
Reza Rassool: And I…

81
00:12:21.740 --> 00:12:29.780
Maira Khwaja: Reza, we also clarified last week that the malice part could be intentional or unintentional, right?

82
00:12:29.780 --> 00:12:37.940
Reza Rassool: Indeed, it could be the fact that, okay, Sam's maybe not an evil person who's intending to

83
00:12:37.940 --> 00:12:50.010
Reza Rassool: resell the data, or train their next model on the data, or mine it for advertising opportunities. They might simply be a service provider that a

84
00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:56.799
Reza Rassool: A state actor could come and compel to reveal the confidential information.

85
00:12:56.910 --> 00:12:59.040
Reza Rassool: And so, they're not…

86
00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:12.389
Reza Rassool: doing anything nefarious, they're just abiding by the law. And so, if you want to make sure that your data, remains secure at a third-party house, you're going to need something like a VPK. Now.

87
00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:24.319
Reza Rassool: I just want to be clear that this is only currently scoped for, making… for enabling confidential search.

88
00:13:24.680 --> 00:13:41.239
Reza Rassool: everyone says, oh, well, I'm sending my data to, my prompts to ChatGPT and, to use their LLM. Yes, you are. Your prompt is in the clear, and we don't yet have a solution for that. So, the scope

89
00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:49.639
Reza Rassool: If we're talking about the scope, we're not scoping, securing, encrypted inference. Encrypted inference is

90
00:13:49.780 --> 00:14:00.739
Reza Rassool: would be icing on the cake for VPK. And we've got some research ideas, but the stuff that we can actually do today is confidential vector search.

91
00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:10.969
Reza Rassool: Okay So, the diagram explains the mechanism for doing that. Let me talk about the scope.

92
00:14:11.310 --> 00:14:27.319
Reza Rassool: In the first meeting last week, we proposed three working groups, which basically breaks the, the, the activities of VPK into three, task forces.

93
00:14:27.490 --> 00:14:38.850
Reza Rassool: So we've got one working group. A working group is supposed to be a long-lived entity, with a, with a continuous mission.

94
00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:51.859
Reza Rassool: it's supposed to spawn task forces which are SMUT, S-M-A-R-T. Forgot what the acronyms stand for, but, it's specific, it's, metrics-based, it's achievable.

95
00:14:51.860 --> 00:14:53.190
Rex Brown: measurable, achievable, yep.

96
00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:57.800
Reza Rassool: Yeah, it's realistic, and it's time-based as well. So…

97
00:14:58.730 --> 00:15:15.300
Reza Rassool: The VPK protocol specification is one of those, sort of, milestones of a task force. And so, the mission we'd send that task force on is to specify and standardize the VPK protocol.

98
00:15:16.520 --> 00:15:23.540
Reza Rassool: If we look at the diagram over here, the trust boundary has moved

99
00:15:23.830 --> 00:15:34.290
Reza Rassool: to the left, or to the right, depending on your point of view, but in the current scheme of things, when you upload a document to Claude, for instance.

100
00:15:34.810 --> 00:15:36.710
Reza Rassool: Claude does the embedding.

101
00:15:38.450 --> 00:15:47.859
Reza Rassool: You upload it in the clear, Claude does the embedding, translates it into some vector space, so it's searchable by Claude's, agents.

102
00:15:48.710 --> 00:15:54.339
Reza Rassool: What we are proposing here with VPK is that the embedding becomes part of Bob's domain.

103
00:15:54.880 --> 00:16:02.929
Reza Rassool: And so, that's important because Bob then has the the vectors…

104
00:16:03.160 --> 00:16:11.060
Reza Rassool: that he then scrambles before uploading it to Sam. Okay. So…

105
00:16:11.790 --> 00:16:18.440
Reza Rassool: A second task force is to research and develop custom vector embedding.

106
00:16:18.860 --> 00:16:30.690
Reza Rassool: Currently, the marketplace for embedders is global embedding. These are embedders where the embedding is

107
00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:36.249
Reza Rassool: is generalized, and it supports all sorts of documents.

108
00:16:37.130 --> 00:16:45.050
Reza Rassool: If the embedder is now part of Bob's domain, and…

109
00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:50.670
Reza Rassool: Sam doesn't need to know what the embedding algorithm is.

110
00:16:50.990 --> 00:16:54.960
Reza Rassool: And that becomes part of Bob's key material.

111
00:16:55.600 --> 00:17:08.449
Reza Rassool: And it's part of Bob's secret. He's customizing the embedder. The side effect of customizing the embedder is it actually improves the semantic efficiency.

112
00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:17.549
Reza Rassool: The semantic efficiency, that means, you can have A lower dimensional vector space.

113
00:17:17.660 --> 00:17:37.000
Reza Rassool: you get a higher level of accuracy, in the translation of the clear text to the vectors, and, so, so searchers can, can find each other. So, so the query can find the, can find the original data through a semantic embedding.

114
00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:41.980
Reza Rassool: And, there's research on that, but we need to…

115
00:17:42.740 --> 00:17:49.949
Reza Rassool: Kick off a task force to show, hey, you can create a personal vector embedding.

116
00:17:50.080 --> 00:18:04.060
Reza Rassool: That vectorizes your documents, or your knowledge base of documents, and it's tuned to that vocabulary, or that semantic… the semantic space of that domain of knowledge.

117
00:18:04.490 --> 00:18:11.340
Reza Rassool: So maybe, Tom, that's part of where your interests lie. It's in personal vector embedding.

118
00:18:11.450 --> 00:18:19.350
Reza Rassool: Okay. The other thing that I need to clarify, and I heard it mentioned,

119
00:18:20.850 --> 00:18:32.830
Reza Rassool: It's not fully homomorphic. Fully homomorphic encryption means that you can do any math operation on the scrambled data. This is partially homomorphic.

120
00:18:33.590 --> 00:18:39.629
Reza Rassool: The thing that you can do on the scramble data is compute the dot product.

121
00:18:39.980 --> 00:18:44.559
Reza Rassool: And you get back the same answer as if

122
00:18:44.790 --> 00:18:48.970
Reza Rassool: It were computed on the unencrypted data.

123
00:18:49.770 --> 00:18:53.859
Reza Rassool: So, I give you a…

124
00:18:54.040 --> 00:19:06.690
Reza Rassool: vector, a list of vectors, unscrambled, and then I have a query, which I vectorize, and I transmit to you, and you use your massive computing

125
00:19:06.690 --> 00:19:18.149
Reza Rassool: Capability to run through the dot product math of a trillion vectors. And you come back with, here are the top 10, hits.

126
00:19:18.750 --> 00:19:23.229
Reza Rassool: That's what a fast vector database service does.

127
00:19:23.550 --> 00:19:33.570
Reza Rassool: Now, if I gave you that data scrambled, and I also scramble the query with the same, key.

128
00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:39.829
Reza Rassool: You'll come back with the same answer as if it was unscrambled.

129
00:19:40.590 --> 00:19:47.280
Reza Rassool: And that is the theory behind partial Homomorphic encryption for vector databases.

130
00:19:47.420 --> 00:19:48.420
Reza Rassool: And…

131
00:19:48.910 --> 00:20:01.919
Reza Rassool: We've shown it to work, and we've, tested it on a number of algorithms. Some of the homegrown algorithms, there's simple dimensional scrambling, there's noise injection, there is,

132
00:20:02.070 --> 00:20:15.260
Reza Rassool: two algorithms that came out of a work group that Kwai commissioned at Pomona College with the Society of Industrial and Applied Mathematics. One is called Rome, the other's called Die Hard.

133
00:20:15.330 --> 00:20:25.689
Reza Rassool: They're kind of medium-strength algorithms, and then there is an algorithm that comes out of the SEAL library, which is called CKKS,

134
00:20:25.730 --> 00:20:37.100
Reza Rassool: which is, Chong, Kim, Kim, and Song. It's, South Korean researchers that actually developed a fully homomorphic, algorithm.

135
00:20:37.760 --> 00:20:42.170
Reza Rassool: Problem with CKKS is it takes an age to compute.

136
00:20:42.280 --> 00:20:45.819
Reza Rassool: So, what we're looking for in

137
00:20:46.050 --> 00:20:54.799
Reza Rassool: the VPK specification is a solution for confidential vector search.

138
00:20:55.030 --> 00:20:58.399
Reza Rassool: That you can run at conversational speed.

139
00:20:58.630 --> 00:21:03.600
Reza Rassool: So… It's got to be something that is good enough.

140
00:21:03.810 --> 00:21:08.209
Reza Rassool: When… we're not looking to, make this…

141
00:21:08.390 --> 00:21:12.660
Reza Rassool: It doesn't have to be, perfect.

142
00:21:13.130 --> 00:21:14.989
Reza Rassool: But it has to be good enough.

143
00:21:15.140 --> 00:21:23.770
Reza Rassool: So that it becomes economically unfeasible for a hacker to, to try and hack it.

144
00:21:24.780 --> 00:21:25.480
Reza Rassool: So…

145
00:21:25.480 --> 00:21:29.200
Rex Brown: And maybe even slightly slower than real time would still be of value, of course, too.

146
00:21:29.750 --> 00:21:47.469
Reza Rassool: Yes, exactly. Slightly slower than real time, yes, please. And try and avoid a scalable hack, where they've hacked it for you, and that hack basically means they've hacked it for everyone. That'd be the worst case. That… so, part of what we'd have to do in…

147
00:21:47.630 --> 00:21:57.039
Reza Rassool: measuring the, the security, the strength of the solution, is to grade it.

148
00:21:57.690 --> 00:22:15.259
Reza Rassool: when I was, chief engineer at Widevine Technologies, and Widevine is the DRM that secures most of internet television at the moment, it secures Netflix, Hulu, Disney, Amazon Prime. It is,

149
00:22:15.930 --> 00:22:27.099
Reza Rassool: there was an audit, an auditing firm that was anointed by the Hollywood studios called Merdan, and they graded, security solutions

150
00:22:27.100 --> 00:22:37.229
Reza Rassool: from, I think they gave them 5 grades. And, we should, we should come… come up with that same level of rigor,

151
00:22:37.280 --> 00:22:43.280
Reza Rassool: And try and grade the, the,

152
00:22:43.650 --> 00:22:55.960
Reza Rassool: attacks, the attack vectors, potential attack vectors, and the, the sort of vulnerabilities that are exposed… are exposed. Is the hacker just,

153
00:22:56.460 --> 00:23:13.080
Reza Rassool: hacking one session, one knowledge base, or the entire, the entire, platform, in the way that,

154
00:23:13.300 --> 00:23:27.270
Reza Rassool: DECSS did to DVDs, when, one, they hacked it once, and that basically meant that all DVDs were cracked, and so we want to avoid situations like that.

155
00:23:28.610 --> 00:23:41.409
Reza Rassool: Part of the way in which we, spread the risk is also through sharding. And so, any scrambling mechanism that you adopt here should be shardable.

156
00:23:42.080 --> 00:23:49.320
Reza Rassool: Which means that you can spread the vector database, your knowledge base, across multiple SAMs.

157
00:23:49.450 --> 00:23:52.259
Reza Rassool: And each one of them could have its own key.

158
00:23:52.390 --> 00:24:04.249
Reza Rassool: So it would require a massive collusion attack for them to, to be able to, reconstruct the entire vector database.

159
00:24:04.740 --> 00:24:16.949
Reza Rassool: The other part of Bob's secret is he can scramble the order of the records in the vector database, not just scramble each vector. So that becomes important because

160
00:24:17.160 --> 00:24:18.760
Reza Rassool: when Sam…

161
00:24:18.970 --> 00:24:27.540
Reza Rassool: When Bob issues the query to Sam, he says, don't give me the answer. Give me the index of the answer.

162
00:24:27.650 --> 00:24:32.669
Reza Rassool: So that type of query to a vector database is called search by index.

163
00:24:33.280 --> 00:24:40.589
Reza Rassool: And so, no clear… no… Scrambled vector makes its way on the return path.

164
00:24:41.030 --> 00:24:45.019
Reza Rassool: So that, that gives it an extra level of,

165
00:24:45.210 --> 00:24:49.270
Reza Rassool: security. Tom, have you heard of… Question?

166
00:24:51.280 --> 00:24:52.190
Reza Rassool: Oh, okay.

167
00:24:52.190 --> 00:24:55.730
Tom Jones: Well, actually, I was sort of typing it out.

168
00:24:56.150 --> 00:24:58.550
Tom Jones: I'm a little unclear on whether or not this

169
00:24:59.420 --> 00:25:07.490
Tom Jones: let me call it a database, I don't know what else to call it. This thing that's created is stored up in the cloud, or is it something that,

170
